      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A post office opened in 1886, with Fredrick Baumgartel as its first postmaster, serving mail on the daily passenger train.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crookston%2C_Nebraska",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "By 1914, Crookston had grown into a thriving community with a school offering grades 1‑10, two restaurants, a movie house, and a bank.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27,
        29,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crookston%2C_Nebraska",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The town sits in the Minnechaduza Valley, once called the Gateway to the Great Rosebud Reservation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.legendsofamerica.com/ne-crookston/",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "As transportation improved in the 20th century, larger nearby towns drew commerce, turning Crookston into a semi‑ghost town.",
